TASK: Write a Fedora KDE Post-Installation Script in Bash

SECTION 1. OBJECTIVE

Write a single, self-contained Bash script named postinstall.sh. It configures a freshly installed Fedora KDE Plasma system (Fedora 41 or newer, Plasma 6) through an interactive ASCII menu where the user toggles modules on or off, then runs only the selected modules.

Output only the complete script in one code block, with no commentary before or after it. Comments inside the script are encouraged.

SECTION 2. EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT (HARD CONSTRAINTS)

- The script is launched from a terminal immediately after the first boot into a new install, with this exact command:
  curl -sO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bitflipkickflip/fedora_postinstall/main/postinstall.sh && bash postinstall.sh
- The script runs as a normal user, not as root. It must never be run with sudo as a whole. It must refuse to run (clear message, non-zero exit) if started as root, because user-scoped actions (home directory files, KDE settings, wallpapers) must apply to the real user.
- Privileged operations use sudo on individual commands only. Per-user operations never use sudo.
- The user must be asked for their sudo password exactly once, after menu selection and before any module runs. The credential must stay valid for the entire run, including long operations such as full system upgrades and kernel module builds. If the script exits for any reason, any helper process used to keep the credential alive must also end and must not be left orphaned.
- All interactive reads must be robust and must read from the controlling terminal device (/dev/tty) explicitly, not from stdin.
- Network access is assumed. A fresh Fedora KDE install has no RPM Fusion, no Flatpak remote configured at system scope, and no third-party repositories. Do not assume any of them exist.
- Fedora uses dnf5 (Fedora 41+). Use command syntax valid for dnf5, particularly for adding repositories from a .repo file and for enabling or disabling existing repositories. Use the Fedora release number detected at runtime rather than hardcoding a version.
- Hardcode no username or home path. Use the invoking user's environment.

SECTION 3. SHELL BEHAVIOR REQUIREMENTS

- The shebang must be #!/bin/bash.
- Enable the -u and pipefail options. Do NOT enable -e: one failed module must not abort the rest of the run, and the user must see which steps failed.
- Quote all variable expansions.
- Use readonly for constants, local for function variables, and associative arrays for option state and labels.
- Use an ordered indexed array of keys so the menu renders in an identical order on every run (associative arrays are unordered).
- Include a short comment above any non-obvious command flag explaining what it does.
- Colored log output with three levels: info (blue), success (green), warning (yellow). Reset color codes after each message.
- Escape codes used with echo -e must not leave stray escape sequences in the output.

SECTION 4. MODULES (ORGANIZED BY CATEGORY)

Implement these 14 modules. Each is independently toggleable. Default states are shown in brackets.

CATEGORY A: APPEARANCE

1. Wallpaper Setup: a multi-choice option (not a boolean). Values: 1 = Standard 1440p (2560x1440) [default], 2 = Ultrawide 1440p (3440x1440), 3 = Skip.
   - Download the matching image from the GitHub repository bitflipkickflip/fedora_postinstall, branch main, directory wallpapers/, into the user's local wallpapers directory (~/.local/share/wallpapers). Create the directory if missing.
   - Filenames: Fedora_GrayBlue_Penguin_2560_1440.png and Fedora_GrayBlue_Penguin_3440_1440.png. Fetch them by raw file URL, following redirects.
   - Apply the image as the desktop wallpaper on all desktops/screens of the running Plasma 6 session by scripting the live plasma shell over D-Bus (use the Qt6 variant of qdbus).
   - Apply the same image as the lock screen (screen locker) wallpaper through the KDE 6 config tooling.
   - Set both default panels to non-floating.
   - Restart plasmashell so changes appear immediately, in a way that returns control to the script and does not tie plasmashell to the script's lifetime (the shell must keep running after the script ends). Suppress its output.
   - This module must run after all package modules so a plasmashell restart does not disrupt installs.

CATEGORY B: SYSTEM

2. System Upgrade [on]: refresh metadata, upgrade all packages non-interactively, then remove orphaned dependencies. Runs last among package-affecting modules.

3. Install NVIDIA Drivers [off]: enable the RPM Fusion nonfree NVIDIA driver repository and install the kernel module package plus the CUDA userspace driver package. This module requires the RPM Fusion free and nonfree release packages to be present (see Section 5).

CATEGORY C: BROWSERS

4. Install Brave [on]: add Brave's official RPM repository from its .repo file at https://brave-browser-rpm-release.s3.brave.com/brave-browser.repo, then install brave-browser. Ensure the dnf plugin package providing repository management is installed first.

5. Remove Firefox [on]: uninstall the Fedora Firefox package. Ordering rule: if both Brave and Remove Firefox are selected, Brave should install before Firefox is removed so the user is never left without a browser. At minimum, do not remove Firefox if the Brave install failed while Brave was selected.

CATEGORY D: GAMING

6. Install Steam [on]: install the Fedora third-party workstation repositories package, enable the RPM Fusion nonfree Steam repository, install steam.

7. Install Prism Launcher [on]: install Flatpak from dnf if missing, add the Flathub remote at system scope (idempotently), install org.prismlauncher.PrismLauncher system-wide, non-interactively.

8. Install Jagex Launcher [on]: download the AppImage from https://rs-launcher-updates.runescape.com/production/linux/x64/latest/jagex-launcher-beta-linux-x86_64.AppImage into ~/.local/bin as jagex-launcher.AppImage (create the directory, follow redirects), mark it executable, then briefly launch it in the background with output suppressed so it can register its desktop integration, wait about 3 seconds, and terminate it gracefully. This step must not hang the script or produce error output if the launcher exits on its own.

CATEGORY E: COMMUNICATION AND MEDIA

9. Install Discord [on]: install the RPM Fusion free and nonfree release packages for the detected Fedora version (if not already installed), then install discord.

10. Install VLC [on]

11. Install Kdenlive [on]

CATEGORY F: PRODUCTIVITY AND CREATIVE

12. Replace KWrite with Sublime Text [on]: remove kwrite, import Sublime HQ's RPM signing key from https://download.sublimetext.com/sublimehq-rpm-pub.gpg, add the stable x86_64 repository from https://download.sublimetext.com/rpm/stable/x86_64/sublime-text.repo, install sublime-text.

13. Install OrcaSlicer [on]: via Flatpak at system scope using application ID com.orcaslicer.OrcaSlicer (same Flathub setup rules as Prism Launcher; do not duplicate remote setup logic in a way that errors on a second run).

CATEGORY G: DEBLOAT

14. Remove KDE Bloat [on]: uninstall these packages non-interactively: kontact, kmail, korganizer, akregator, kaddressbook, kcontacts, kaccounts-integration, kaccounts-providers, akonadi-server, akonadi-contacts, akonadi-calendar, kdepim-addons, akonadiconsole, itinerary, elisa-player, dragon, kamoso, kate, khelpcenter, kfind, konqueror, neochat, kpatience, kmines, ksudoku, kinfocenter, kpat, kmahjongg.
   - Then autoremove orphans.
   - Then delete leftover per-user config, data, and cache entries whose names case-insensitively match kontact, kmail, akonadi, korganizer, elisa, kaccounts, or neochat under the user's ~/.config, ~/.local/share, and ~/.cache.
   - A package in the list that is not installed must not stop removal of the others.
   - Runs first among package-affecting modules.

SECTION 5. DEPENDENCY AND ORDERING RULES

Package managers hold a transaction lock and repositories must exist before packages can be installed from them, so:

- Execution order: Debloat, Browsers, NVIDIA, Gaming, Communication/Media/Productivity, Wallpaper, System Upgrade, reboot prompt. The upgrade must not run midway through other installs.
- RPM Fusion must be configured before any module depending on it (NVIDIA, Steam, Discord, VLC). Configure it exactly once per run, lazily and idempotently, when the first dependent module is selected. Do not install it if no dependent module is selected.
- Never run two package-manager commands concurrently.
- Re-running the script on a partially configured system must not fail because a repo, remote, or directory already exists.
- A failed step must produce a visible warning naming the module, then execution continues. Track failures and print a summary of failed modules at the end.

SECTION 6. INTERACTIVE MENU SPECIFICATION

Render with clear and plain ASCII only (no Unicode box characters). Layout, in order:

1. A banner of = characters containing the title "FEDORA POST-INSTALLATION CONFIGURATOR" centered.
2. A column header row: "Module / Option" and "Current Status", followed by a - rule.
3. One row per module, numbered 1 to 14, grouped under category headings (Appearance, System, Browsers, Gaming, Communication & Media, Productivity & Creative, Debloat). Numbering must be continuous across categories. Each row has the label left-aligned in a fixed-width column, then a colon, then the status.
4. Status display: booleans show ON or OFF (colorized green/red is welcome, but alignment must not break because of invisible escape codes). The wallpaper row shows "Resolution 2560x1440", "Resolution 3440x1440", or "Disabled".
5. A help block explaining the wallpaper values.
6. Instructions: enter a module number to toggle it; "run" or "r" to execute; "quit" or "q" to abort.

Input behavior:
- Prompt text: "set > ".
- Input is case-insensitive, trimmed of whitespace, and tolerant of Windows carriage returns.
- A number toggles that module. For the wallpaper module it instead shows a short sub-prompt asking for 1, 2, or 3 and sets the value only if valid.
- Out-of-range numbers and unrecognized text are silently ignored and the menu redraws.
- quit or q prints an abort message and exits with status 0 without prompting for a password or changing anything.
- run or r leaves the menu loop.
- All reads use the terminal device, not stdin.

SECTION 7. POST-RUN BEHAVIOR

1. Print a clear completion banner, plus the failure summary if any modules failed.
2. Ask: "Would you like to restart your system now? [Y/n]:". Empty input, y, yes, or 1 means yes. Anything else means no.
3. If yes: before rebooting, wait until no dnf or akmods process is still running (the NVIDIA kernel module may still be building in the background), printing a warning every 5 seconds while waiting. Then reboot with sudo.
4. If no: print a message reminding the user to reboot manually, especially if NVIDIA drivers were installed.
5. In both branches, the script deletes its own file (it was downloaded to the current directory by the curl command and is referenced via the script's own path) so no installer is left behind. Handle paths safely, including a path with a leading dash.

SECTION 8. QUALITY REQUIREMENTS

- Zero use of eval.
- The script must pass bash -n (syntax check) and be clean under shellcheck at default severity, aside from justified disables with comments.
- No hardcoded Fedora version numbers.
- Do not use features unavailable on a stock Fedora KDE install. Assume only coreutils, curl, dnf, sudo, and KDE Plasma 6 tools are present. Do not assume dialog, whiptail, git, or wget.
- No heredocs with unquoted terminators that could expand unintended variables.
- Total length is not constrained, but keep it readable and organized into clearly separated sections: Display/Logging, State, Menu, Execution Modules, Main.

SECTION 9. EVALUATION RUBRIC (100 POINTS)

- 10 points: Runs as normal user, rejects root, reads from /dev/tty, bash -n passes.
- 20 points: Menu is ASCII only, ordered, categorized, aligned; toggles and wallpaper multi-choice work; quit exits cleanly with no sudo prompt.
- 15 points: Sudo prompted exactly once, credential stays alive through the run, helper cleaned up on exit.
- 15 points: Correct dnf5-valid syntax; repo setup (RPM Fusion, Brave, Sublime, Flathub at system scope); Fedora version detected dynamically.
- 10 points: Module ordering, dependency handling, idempotency, no concurrent package operations.
- 10 points: Wallpaper (desktop and lock screen), non-floating panels, plasmashell restart that survives script exit.
- 5 points: Failure handling: continues on error, tracks and summarizes failures, Firefox not removed if Brave failed.
- 10 points: Reboot flow: waits for dnf/akmods, self-deletes in both branches, safe path handling.
- 5 points: Code quality: quoting, local, readonly, comments on flags, shellcheck-clean.

Penalties: any bash-incompatible syntax, any use of eval, running the whole script as root, prompting for a password more than once, or including text outside the single code block.
